WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.114 --> 00:00:02.260
[crying]

00:00:02.260 --> 00:00:05.630
Narrator: This crying
woman isn't crying at all.

00:00:05.630 --> 00:00:06.890
Elaine Hall: And a baby'll start to cry.

00:00:06.890 --> 00:00:09.680
Narrator: She's what's
known as a baby wrangler,

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:11.240
someone whose job it is

00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:15.660
to get the best performances
out of the youngest actors.

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:18.550
This is just one of the
many surprising positions

00:00:18.550 --> 00:00:21.661
you will find on a movie or TV set.

00:00:21.661 --> 00:00:22.930
Cameraman: Whoa!

00:00:22.930 --> 00:00:25.280
Narrator: There are
people who train insects,

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:26.955
style food,

00:00:26.955 --> 00:00:30.460
and even design clothes
for animated films.

00:00:30.460 --> 00:00:33.940
These jobs are essential
to making movies work.

00:00:33.940 --> 00:00:35.890
Let's take a look behind the scenes

00:00:35.890 --> 00:00:40.056
at eight jobs in movies you
may not have known existed.

00:00:43.450 --> 00:00:47.120
It's not only real actors
who need costume designers,

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:49.320
but animated characters as well.

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:51.110
At Pixar, there are people

00:00:51.110 --> 00:00:54.090
who are specifically
hired to animate clothing,

00:00:54.090 --> 00:00:57.290
like tailoring and simulation
artist Kris Campbell.

00:00:57.290 --> 00:00:59.960
They have to design and model clothes

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:03.260
and then make sure they fit
on the animated characters.

00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:06.290
This process involves computer simulations

00:01:06.290 --> 00:01:08.830
to make sure the fabric moves correctly.

00:01:08.830 --> 00:01:10.440
In "Coco," for example,

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:13.270
these simulations helped
them prevent clothes

00:01:13.270 --> 00:01:16.470
from getting stuck in the
skeleton characters' bones.

00:01:16.470 --> 00:01:18.330
Campbell's work throughout the years

00:01:18.330 --> 00:01:20.070
prepared him for "Onward."

00:01:20.070 --> 00:01:23.280
However, he wasn't just
animating clothes this time.

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:27.000
In this film, the item of
clothing was a character.

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:30.140
Acting out how the animated
characters move in real life

00:01:30.140 --> 00:01:31.500
can provide reference.

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:34.010
When the animators in
"Onward" needed to figure out

00:01:34.010 --> 00:01:36.630
how a floating pair of pants would behave,

00:01:36.630 --> 00:01:40.340
they put someone in a green
suit and a pair of khaki pants

00:01:40.340 --> 00:01:41.810
and observed what it would look like

00:01:41.810 --> 00:01:43.570
to see somebody walking around

00:01:43.570 --> 00:01:46.120
who was missing the
top half of their body.

00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:48.890
While a clothing animator
focuses on clothing,

00:01:48.890 --> 00:01:50.910
they still have a great understanding

00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:53.880
of how every aspect of animation works.

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:57.810
Dad's upper half in "Onward"
had no muscle underneath,

00:01:57.810 --> 00:02:00.420
making simulations much more challenging.

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:03.010
So Campbell relied on a simulation created

00:02:03.010 --> 00:02:04.800
for characters with volume,

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:07.460
which was originally used
for the squishy movements

00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:10.360
of Hank the octopus in "Finding Dory."

00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:13.380
And they need to add
small details to clothes

00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:15.300
that you would normally expect to see,

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:18.030
like little threads hanging off a sweater.

00:02:18.030 --> 00:02:21.790
A vital but unexpected
part of the job: shading.

00:02:21.790 --> 00:02:24.650
Lighting can help textures
look more realistic,

00:02:24.650 --> 00:02:26.560
which can sell the whole look.

00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:28.650
This is the reason why Dad's vest

00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:31.750
looks naturally puffy instead of plastic.

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:38.710
Productions try to use real
food whenever they can,

00:02:38.710 --> 00:02:39.832
and when they do,

00:02:39.832 --> 00:02:42.450
a food stylist comes in to handle it all.

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:45.030
It's a lot more than just altering dishes

00:02:45.030 --> 00:02:47.460
to fit an actor's dietary restrictions,

00:02:47.460 --> 00:02:49.930
though that is certainly part of the job.

00:02:49.930 --> 00:02:51.940
Food stylist Zoe Hegedus

00:02:51.940 --> 00:02:54.310
had to solve a number
of different problems

00:02:54.310 --> 00:02:55.850
on the set of "Midsommar."

00:02:55.850 --> 00:02:59.050
The film was mostly shot
outdoors in the heat,

00:02:59.050 --> 00:03:00.830
so she had to make sure the food

00:03:00.830 --> 00:03:03.400
both looked and stayed fresh.

00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:05.100
Sometimes, this was as easy

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:07.670
as spraying a potato with cooking spray

00:03:07.670 --> 00:03:10.340
to make it look like it
just came out of the oven.

00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:13.930
Other dishes needed to be altered
entirely to beat the heat.

00:03:13.930 --> 00:03:17.110
For example, those meat
pies you see on screen

00:03:17.110 --> 00:03:20.380
aren't filled with real meat,
which would spoil quickly.

00:03:20.380 --> 00:03:24.420
Instead, Hegedus cooked
oatmeal and colored it brown,

00:03:24.420 --> 00:03:27.860
giving it the look and
texture of chopped meat.

00:03:27.860 --> 00:03:31.340
She also had to plate a lot
of eggs for the Sun's Eye dish

00:03:31.340 --> 00:03:32.790
that was prominently featured

00:03:32.790 --> 00:03:34.953
during one of the film's many feasts.

00:03:34.953 --> 00:03:37.662
First, she tried making some real eggs,

00:03:37.662 --> 00:03:39.810
but the yolks kept exploding,

00:03:39.810 --> 00:03:41.030
meaning she and her team

00:03:41.030 --> 00:03:44.280
had to swap the dishes
out every 15 minutes.

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:47.510
Plus, workdays could last 10 to 12 hours,

00:03:47.510 --> 00:03:49.770
so there was a risk of them drying out.

00:03:49.770 --> 00:03:52.480
Instead, she decided to make this mixture

00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:54.540
out of orange and mango juice

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:57.890
and shaped it to look
exactly like real yolks.

00:03:57.890 --> 00:03:59.820
And what does a food stylist do

00:03:59.820 --> 00:04:01.160
when they have to find an item

00:04:01.160 --> 00:04:03.520
that's not even available
where they're filming?

00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:05.010
For one pivotal moment,

00:04:05.010 --> 00:04:07.040
they needed to have a fresh herring,

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:08.860
which is abundant in Sweden

00:04:08.860 --> 00:04:11.950
but not so much in Hungary,
where the movie was shot.

00:04:11.950 --> 00:04:13.430
The fish had to be real

00:04:13.430 --> 00:04:17.050
in order to get this horrified
reaction from Florence Pugh.

00:04:17.050 --> 00:04:20.070
Eventually, she had to
get 20 pieces of herring

00:04:20.070 --> 00:04:21.700
shipped in from France,

00:04:21.700 --> 00:04:24.760
meaning they had only 20
tries to get it right.

00:04:24.760 --> 00:04:28.160
Luckily, they nailed it
after seven or eight tries.

00:04:31.650 --> 00:04:33.680
Some foods are just too difficult

00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:35.630
to use the real thing on set.

00:04:35.630 --> 00:04:39.460
For example, real ice cream
would melt too quickly.

00:04:39.460 --> 00:04:41.140
Or maybe you need a lot of food

00:04:41.140 --> 00:04:43.120
to sit in the background of a shot.

00:04:43.120 --> 00:04:46.720
In these instances, a
fake-food artist is essential.

00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:49.444
This scene in "Glee" was set in a diner

00:04:49.444 --> 00:04:51.710
and called for lots of ice cream sundaes,

00:04:51.710 --> 00:04:55.110
milkshakes, and banana splits
on the tables and counters.

00:04:55.110 --> 00:04:56.640
Fake versions were needed

00:04:56.640 --> 00:04:59.560
so they would appear fresh
and new the entire time.

00:04:59.560 --> 00:05:01.570
Fake-food artist Brenda Chapman

00:05:01.570 --> 00:05:03.700
made a variety of these desserts,

00:05:03.700 --> 00:05:06.530
which were set at various
stages of being eaten.

00:05:06.530 --> 00:05:09.800
When someone like Lisa Friedman
needs to make fake food,

00:05:09.800 --> 00:05:11.420
she'll first get a real version

00:05:11.420 --> 00:05:13.460
of the food she needs to recreate.

00:05:13.460 --> 00:05:15.910
Then she makes a mold of that item.

00:05:15.910 --> 00:05:18.840
She pours a mixture of rubber and foam

00:05:18.840 --> 00:05:21.880
into that same mold to create the shape,

00:05:21.880 --> 00:05:24.630
but it isn't until the
item is hand-painted

00:05:24.630 --> 00:05:27.420
that it starts to look
like the real thing.

00:05:27.420 --> 00:05:29.460
Aside from being a great painter,

00:05:29.460 --> 00:05:31.010
being a fake-food artist

00:05:31.010 --> 00:05:33.430
means getting creative with the materials.

00:05:33.430 --> 00:05:35.700
To make granola and ground beef,

00:05:35.700 --> 00:05:38.070
Friedman took corkboard and crushed it,

00:05:38.070 --> 00:05:40.403
giving her just the right texture.

00:05:44.620 --> 00:05:47.940
Sometimes, a character will
need different-looking eyes

00:05:47.940 --> 00:05:49.900
than the performer portraying them,

00:05:49.900 --> 00:05:54.250
such as Hugh Jackman's bloodshot
eyes in "Les Misérables."

00:05:54.250 --> 00:05:55.480
In these cases,

00:05:55.480 --> 00:05:58.820
they'll go somewhere like
Eyeworks for Film in London.

00:05:58.820 --> 00:06:02.130
Putting contacts on can
completely change the color

00:06:02.130 --> 00:06:03.600
of an actor's eyes.

00:06:03.600 --> 00:06:05.780
It can also make them look older

00:06:05.780 --> 00:06:08.040
or even like another species.

00:06:08.040 --> 00:06:10.780
First, the designer will
get a brief description

00:06:10.780 --> 00:06:11.700
of the character,

00:06:11.700 --> 00:06:14.450
then they'll research what
the look should look like,

00:06:14.450 --> 00:06:15.778
develop concept art,

00:06:15.778 --> 00:06:17.930
and then finally check the actor's eyes

00:06:17.930 --> 00:06:19.540
to fit the contacts for them.

00:06:19.540 --> 00:06:20.730
In order for the contacts

00:06:20.730 --> 00:06:23.020
to feel normal and natural to wear,

00:06:23.020 --> 00:06:26.050
director of Eyeworks
for Film Sinead Sweeney

00:06:26.050 --> 00:06:28.160
says the contacts they design

00:06:28.160 --> 00:06:31.010
have to sit directly on top of the cornea.

00:06:31.010 --> 00:06:34.070
They have to make lenses
of all shapes and sizes.

00:06:34.070 --> 00:06:35.680
Sometimes, the lenses they make

00:06:35.680 --> 00:06:38.060
are much bigger than the average pupil.

00:06:38.060 --> 00:06:41.390
The trick to getting them
on? As Eyeworks told Insider,

00:06:41.390 --> 00:06:44.523
put your chin up and turn
your eyes to the left.

00:06:48.070 --> 00:06:51.520
Working with bugs on
camera is no easy feat,

00:06:51.520 --> 00:06:55.070
but, yes, you can train an insect to act.

00:06:55.070 --> 00:06:57.340
Getting bugs to move
in the right direction

00:06:57.340 --> 00:07:01.320
involves playing with light,
air, and room temperature.

00:07:01.320 --> 00:07:03.080
Entomologist Steven Kutcher

00:07:03.080 --> 00:07:06.270
can get all kinds of bugs
to perform on camera.

00:07:06.270 --> 00:07:08.600
For "Spider-Man" and "Arachnophobia,"

00:07:08.600 --> 00:07:11.390
he was hired to wrangle
the film's spiders.

00:07:11.390 --> 00:07:13.520
For one shot in "Arachnophobia,"

00:07:13.520 --> 00:07:16.110
a spider needed to crawl across the floor

00:07:16.110 --> 00:07:17.610
and into a slipper.

00:07:17.610 --> 00:07:19.970
So Kutcher devised a clever trick

00:07:19.970 --> 00:07:22.330
using invisible vibrating wires.

00:07:22.330 --> 00:07:23.950
By playing around with these,

00:07:23.950 --> 00:07:25.410
Kutcher could get the spiders

00:07:25.410 --> 00:07:27.550
directly where they needed to go.

00:07:27.550 --> 00:07:29.070
When working on "Spider Man,"

00:07:29.070 --> 00:07:31.820
Kutcher had to get a spider
to descend from a web

00:07:31.820 --> 00:07:34.210
and then onto Tobey Maguire's hand,

00:07:34.210 --> 00:07:36.660
where it could deliver the fateful bite.

00:07:36.660 --> 00:07:39.210
Kutcher decided to stand above Maguire

00:07:39.210 --> 00:07:40.980
while holding a paintbrush.

00:07:40.980 --> 00:07:43.108
The spider crawled along the paintbrush

00:07:43.108 --> 00:07:44.620
until it reached the end.

00:07:44.620 --> 00:07:47.500
At that point, Kutcher tapped the brush,

00:07:47.500 --> 00:07:49.780
and the spider dropped down.

00:07:49.780 --> 00:07:51.830
He faced an even bigger challenge

00:07:51.830 --> 00:07:54.070
in "Exorcist II: The Heretic."

00:07:54.070 --> 00:07:57.040
He had to train 3,000 locusts,

00:07:57.040 --> 00:08:00.390
which needed to cling onto
the screened wall of a cage.

00:08:00.390 --> 00:08:03.330
However, most of these
locusts stood on the floor.

00:08:03.330 --> 00:08:05.460
In order to drive them onto the walls,

00:08:05.460 --> 00:08:07.320
he took a heat-emitting light

00:08:07.320 --> 00:08:09.108
and shined it against the cage,

00:08:09.108 --> 00:08:12.083
and the locusts subsequently
flocked towards it.

00:08:15.600 --> 00:08:18.890
When a sex scene is filmed
for a movie or TV show,

00:08:18.890 --> 00:08:21.680
it is crucial that the
actors feel protected

00:08:21.680 --> 00:08:23.600
and not violated on set.

00:08:23.600 --> 00:08:26.740
For this reason, the role
of intimacy coordinator,

00:08:26.740 --> 00:08:29.020
which is relatively new to Hollywood,

00:08:29.020 --> 00:08:30.762
has proven to be essential.

00:08:30.762 --> 00:08:33.500
An intimacy coordinator, like Ita O'Brien,

00:08:33.500 --> 00:08:37.350
is tasked with coaching
actors on sex and love scenes.

00:08:37.350 --> 00:08:39.130
The most crucial part of her job

00:08:39.130 --> 00:08:41.670
is making sure these
moments are done safely

00:08:41.670 --> 00:08:44.670
and with clear consent during this time.

00:08:44.670 --> 00:08:48.830
The motivations behind these
scenes are clearly established.

00:08:48.830 --> 00:08:50.280
O'Brien worked with the cast

00:08:50.280 --> 00:08:52.640
of the Netflix series "Sex Education."

00:08:52.640 --> 00:08:54.960
She had to make sure the cast was safe

00:08:54.960 --> 00:08:57.620
and prepared for the
show's intimate moments.

00:08:57.620 --> 00:09:00.430
For these scenes, she created cushions

00:09:00.430 --> 00:09:02.580
and flesh-colored articles of clothing.

00:09:02.580 --> 00:09:06.120
These items gave the cast the
ability to cover themselves

00:09:06.120 --> 00:09:08.360
as well as extra barrier of protection

00:09:08.360 --> 00:09:10.527
during the simulated sex scenes.

00:09:16.040 --> 00:09:18.940
Babies don't always cry on command,

00:09:18.940 --> 00:09:21.110
so when you need one to wail on camera,

00:09:21.110 --> 00:09:23.220
that's when you bring in a professional.

00:09:23.220 --> 00:09:25.410
Elaine Hall is a baby wrangler,

00:09:25.410 --> 00:09:28.560
but she also describes
herself as an interpreter.

00:09:28.560 --> 00:09:30.170
Unlike with adult actors,

00:09:30.170 --> 00:09:33.680
babies can only be on set
for a limited amount of time,

00:09:33.680 --> 00:09:37.250
as they can only legally
work for four hours a day.

00:09:37.250 --> 00:09:39.450
This means that Hall needs to get babies

00:09:39.450 --> 00:09:42.660
to master their scenes in
as few takes as possible.

00:09:42.660 --> 00:09:45.360
She has many tricks for how to do this.

00:09:45.360 --> 00:09:48.380
If she needs to make a young
actor look happy in a scene,

00:09:48.380 --> 00:09:51.740
she might attach a funny-looking
face to the camera lens.

00:09:51.740 --> 00:09:53.930
But what about making a baby cry,

00:09:53.930 --> 00:09:56.582
like she did for a scene
in "Akeelah and the Bee"?

00:09:56.582 --> 00:09:58.740
[baby crying]

00:09:58.740 --> 00:10:02.147
Well, she got really,
really involved and even

00:10:02.147 --> 00:10:03.980
started sobbing herself.
[crying]

00:10:03.980 --> 00:10:05.230
According to Hall,
[crying]

00:10:05.230 --> 00:10:07.140
when the baby heard her crying,

00:10:07.140 --> 00:10:09.990
the baby immediately
started crying herself.

00:10:09.990 --> 00:10:12.040
Then, to get the baby to stop,

00:10:12.040 --> 00:10:14.710
Hall stopped crying and reassured the baby

00:10:14.710 --> 00:10:17.110
that everything was OK.

00:10:17.110 --> 00:10:21.177
Oh, and objects like a
rattle or a bell also help.

00:10:24.720 --> 00:10:27.553
While actors are sometimes
given rubber swords,

00:10:27.553 --> 00:10:29.860
especially for really dangerous stunts,

00:10:29.860 --> 00:10:32.190
there are times when
they need real weapons

00:10:32.190 --> 00:10:34.620
for utmost authenticity on camera.

00:10:34.620 --> 00:10:36.670
Enter blacksmith Tony Swatton,

00:10:36.670 --> 00:10:39.110
who's worked on over 200 movies.

00:10:39.110 --> 00:10:41.370
Yes, he is a real blacksmith

00:10:41.370 --> 00:10:44.645
who makes actual, functioning
swords for Hollywood.

00:10:46.755 --> 00:10:48.187
Cameraman: Whoa!

00:10:48.187 --> 00:10:50.810
Narrator: Swatton's weapons
look great in close-ups

00:10:50.810 --> 00:10:53.902
and give the actors the feeling
of holding a real weapon,

00:10:53.902 --> 00:10:57.030
which in turn makes their
performances even better.

00:10:57.030 --> 00:10:58.710
His job isn't as easy

00:10:58.710 --> 00:11:01.320
as just creating any
old weapon out of metal.

00:11:01.320 --> 00:11:04.310
He needs to be really
accurate to match the setting.

00:11:04.310 --> 00:11:07.120
For instance, when he worked
on "The Mask of Zorro,"

00:11:07.120 --> 00:11:09.280
he created a curved hunting blade

00:11:09.280 --> 00:11:11.446
that would have been used in the 1500s

00:11:11.446 --> 00:11:13.137
for Antonio Banderas.

00:11:13.137 --> 00:11:14.950
Besides being hyper-specific,

00:11:14.950 --> 00:11:17.730
he also needs to make
multiple versions of a weapon

00:11:17.730 --> 00:11:18.950
for different purposes.

00:11:18.950 --> 00:11:22.590
For example, while he will make
a steel sword for close-ups,

00:11:22.590 --> 00:11:24.060
swords made out of aluminum

00:11:24.060 --> 00:11:26.340
are better for sequences involving action

00:11:26.340 --> 00:11:28.010
and actual sword fights.

00:11:28.010 --> 00:11:31.120
You can see here with the
steel blade Swatton made

00:11:31.120 --> 00:11:33.440
for Johnny Depp in
"Pirates of the Caribbean."

00:11:33.440 --> 00:11:35.280
Swatton has worked on some movies

00:11:35.280 --> 00:11:37.610
that involved large battle sequences,

00:11:37.610 --> 00:11:39.230
like "The Last Samurai."

00:11:39.230 --> 00:11:42.866
For that, he wasn't just
making weapons for Tom Cruise;

00:11:42.866 --> 00:11:46.330
he also had to make
about 600 aluminum blades

00:11:46.330 --> 00:11:49.990
for all of the extras in
the biggest battle scenes.

00:11:52.360 --> 00:11:53.760
Ian Phillips: Thanks for watching.

00:11:53.760 --> 00:11:55.940
It takes a whole lot of talented people

00:11:55.940 --> 00:11:58.240
to make your favorite movies.

00:11:58.240 --> 00:12:00.730
What other movies would you
like to learn more about?

00:12:00.730 --> 00:12:02.715
Let us know in the comments.

